==== Grip Strength Pattern

A revealing finding: maximum grip strength showed no difference between patients and controls, but sustained grip strength was markedly reduced. The authors noted: “If deconditioning were the cause, we would expect maximum strength differences” @walitt2024deep—arguing against simple deconditioning as explanation.


*Reconciliation with later cohorts.* The NIH observation concerns *absolute* maximum force in a small post-infectious cohort (n=17). Two larger cohorts report lower absolute grip force in ME/CFS than in controls: 105 adults meeting CCC criteria versus 66 controls @Jakel2021HGS, and 147 children and young adults versus 83 controls (mean difference −9.93 kg, 95% CI −12.00 to −7.85) @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP. The discrepancy is not resolved by the published data: cohort size, diagnostic criteria (post-infectious NIH cohort versus CCC), age range, and dynamometer protocol all differ. What the three studies agree on is narrower and more robust — a *dynamic* deficit. Sustained or repeated grip declines faster in ME/CFS than in controls, whether the baseline maximum is preserved (NIH, n=17) or reduced (Munich cohorts). That convergence is summarised at @cf:handgrip-force-deficit, and the unresolved mechanism is at @oq:handgrip-peripheral-vs-central. Absolute-force findings should therefore not be presented as a single established fact; the direction of the absolute-force difference depends on the cohort.

